---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import ApiName from '@/components/ApiName.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Reveal from '@/components/motion/scenes/Reveal.astro';
import { RevealControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/RevealControls';
import { MotionRevealReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-reveal.tsx';
import { MotionRevealAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-reveal.ts';
import MotionRevealSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-reveal.svelte';

const defineRiseAgain = `import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

// Each entry plays the reveal again: the element goes back to its first frame when it leaves the view.
defineMotion('rise-again', {
  verb: 'reveal',
  keyframes: [{ opacity: 0, y: 24 }, { opacity: 1, y: 0 }],
  stagger: 'normal',
  repeat: 'always',
});`;
---
<MotionPage
  slug="reveal"
  title="Motion: reveal on scroll"
  description="Reveal an element when it enters the view, in HTML, React, Angular and Svelte: it waits in its first frame, then plays once, or on each entry with repeat."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <ForFramework as="span" slot="summary">
    <Fragment slot="html">Put <code>data-motion</code> on an element: it waits in its first frame until it scrolls into view, then plays once.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">Render an element as a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> with the name of a reveal: it waits in its first frame until it enters the view, then plays once.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">Put <code>udxMotion</code> on an element: it waits in its first frame until it enters the view, then plays once.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">Attach <code>motion()</code> to an element: it waits in its first frame until it enters the view, then plays once.</Fragment>
  </ForFramework>

  <MotionStage scene="reveal" label="Live example: Reveal on scroll" height={368}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><RevealControls client:visible size="page" /></Fragment>
    <Reveal size="page" />
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>Reveal an element when it enters the view</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <Fragment slot="html">
      <p>Put <code>data-motion="rise"</code> on the element, and call <code>connectMotion()</code> once for the page. Then:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>An element out of view waits in its first frame, hidden, until it scrolls into view. Then it plays once.</li>
        <li>An element already in view when the page connects stays in its natural state: nothing plays.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <p>Render the element as a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> with <code>name="rise"</code>, and set <code>as</code> to the element you need, such as <code>li</code>. Then:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>An element out of view waits in its first frame until it enters the view. Then it plays once.</li>
        <li>An element already in view when it mounts stays in its natural state: nothing plays.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">
      <p>Add <code>Motion</code> to your component's <code>imports</code>, and put <code>udxMotion="rise"</code> on the element. Then:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>An element out of view waits in its first frame until it enters the view. Then it plays once.</li>
        <li>An element already in view when Angular creates it stays in its natural state: nothing plays.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">
      <p>Import <code>motion</code> from <code>@udixio/ui-svelte</code>, and attach it to the element with <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('rise')&#125;</code>. Then:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>An element out of view waits in its first frame until it enters the view. Then it plays once.</li>
        <li>An element already in view when Svelte mounts it stays in its natural state: nothing plays.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    The live example uses the preset <code>rise</code>: each card fades in while rising 24 px, and the cards that enter the
    view together start one step apart.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-reveal" previewHeight={[424, 400]}>
    <MotionRevealReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionRevealAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionRevealSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>
  <p>
    A reveal counts its element as in view while any part of it shows above the bottom tenth of the viewport. The view is
    also clipped by the boxes that scroll around the element, so a card in a feed plays when the feed shows it.
  </p>

  <h2>Play the reveal again on each entry</h2>
  <p>
    Give the definition <code>repeat: 'always'</code>. Each time the element enters the view, the reveal plays from its first
    frame, and when the element leaves the view, it goes back to its first frame. Define it once, in a module that runs before
    the element is bound, then use its name as you use the name of a preset:
  </p>
  <Code data-code={defineRiseAgain} data-language="ts" />
  <p>
    The name goes where the preset went: <ApiName
      html={'data-motion="rise-again"'}
      react={'<Motion name="rise-again">'}
      angular={'udxMotion="rise-again"'}
      svelte="motion('rise-again')"
    />.
  </p>

  <h2>Replay a reveal from script</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">To replay a reveal from its first frame, call <code>play()</code> on its binding: <code>getMotion(element, 'view')?.play()</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion</code>.</p>
    <p slot="react">Give the <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> a <code>ref</code>. From a handler, call <code>getMotion(element, 'view')?.play()</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion</code>. The <code>play()</code> that <code>useMotion</code> returns replays the reveal too.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Get the element with <code>@ViewChild</code>, and call <code>getMotion(element, 'view')?.play()</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion</code>. The <code>play()</code> of the directive replays the reveal too.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Take the element with <code>bind:this</code>, and call <code>getMotion(element, 'view')?.play()</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <p>
    <code>stagger</code> sets the step between the elements that enter the view together: they start in visual order, one step
    apart. <code>origin</code> sets where that cascade starts. <code>axis</code> makes the reveal watch the horizontal view, for
    a row of cards that scrolls sideways.
  </p>
  <MotionOptions verb="reveal" />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>Without a timing, a reveal moves and fades on the default spatial spring. The <code>rise</code> preset cascades its elements by the normal stagger.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'stagger.normal']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Under reduced motion, a reveal shows its element at once, in its end state, when the element enters the view. This is the default, <code>reduced: 'instant'</code>.</li>
    <li>To fade the element in instead, set <code>reduced: 'fade'</code>. Only the opacity changes, and nothing moves.</li>
    <li>A reveal that is running when reduced motion turns on jumps to its end.</li>
    <li>With <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the element or on an ancestor, the element shows at once, whatever the policy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To set the policy, see <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>.</p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If the browser has no <code>IntersectionObserver</code>, the element is shown in its end state at once, and nothing plays.</li>
    <li>If an element must keep its reveal once it has played, leave <code>repeat</code> at <code>once</code>: an <code>always</code> reveal hides the element again when it leaves the view, even while it is still revealing.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
